</div>
<h2 id="choosing-heading">How to Choose a Conveyor Manufacturer</h2>
<h3>Start With Your Application, Not the Manufacturer</h3>
<p>Define your requirements before contacting vendors: product dimensions and weight, throughput rate, environment (ambient, washdown, cleanroom, outdoor), regulatory requirements (FDA, USDA, ATEX, Class 100), and integration points with upstream and downstream equipment. A clear specification reduces quote variance and helps you compare vendors on equal terms. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">cost guide</a> for factors that drive pricing.</p>
<h3>Evaluate Industry Experience</h3>
<p>A manufacturer that has built 200 <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/">bakery conveyors</a> will anticipate problems, flour dust ingress, high-temperature exposure, sanitation protocols, that a general-purpose builder may not. Ask for case studies or references in your specific industry. If the manufacturer cannot provide them, that does not disqualify them, but it means you should scrutinize their design more carefully.</p>
<h3>Consider Total Cost of Ownership</h3>
<p>The purchase price is one component of conveyor cost. Long-term costs include energy consumption, <a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">replacement parts</a> (belts, rollers, bearings, motors), maintenance labor, and downtime. A conveyor with standard catalog parts from multiple suppliers will cost less to maintain than one with proprietary components available only from the OEM. Ask manufacturers what percentage of wear parts are standard versus proprietary.</p>
<h3>Geographic Proximity Matters</h3>
<p>For installation support, emergency service, and parts delivery, proximity reduces response time and travel costs. This does not mean you must buy local, many manufacturers service nationally, but a company with a service center within a few hours of your facility can respond to breakdowns faster. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-companies/">conveyor companies near you</a> guide for regional search strategies.</p>
<h3>Get Multiple Quotes</h3>
<p>Request quotes from at least 2-3 manufacturers. Ensure all vendors are quoting on the same specification: same conveyor type, length, width, speed, load capacity, material of construction, controls scope, and installation scope. Price differences of 20-40% between qualified vendors are common and reflect differences in manufacturing approach, overhead structure, and local market conditions, not necessarily quality differences.</p>
<h2 id="associations-heading">Industry Associations &amp; Directories</h2>
<p>Beyond this directory, the following industry associations maintain member directories that can help you identify conveyor manufacturers for specific applications:</p>
<h3><a href="https://www.cema.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CEMA</a></h3>
<p>Conveyor Equipment Manufacturers Association, trade association for North American conveyor manufacturers. Member directory organized by conveyor type.</p>
<h3><a href="https://www.mhi.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MHI</a></h3>
<p>Material Handling Industry, parent organization covering conveyors, AGVs, robotics, and warehouse automation. Hosts ProMat and MODEX trade shows.</p>
<h3><a href="https://www.pmmi.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PMMI</a></h3>
<p>Association for Packaging and Processing Technologies, members include packaging machinery and conveyor manufacturers. Hosts PACK EXPO trade shows.</p>
<h3>Thomas</h3>
<p>Thomasnet.com, industrial supplier directory with detailed company profiles, certifications, and capabilities filtering for conveyor manufacturers.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">Who are the largest conveyor manufacturers?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Major conveyor manufacturers include Hytrol, Dorner, Interroll, mk North America, Bastian Solutions (now a Toyota Advanced Logistics company), Dematic, Honeywell Intelligrated, and Siemens Logistics. In food and packaging specifically, Key Technology, Heat and Control, Intralox (a Laitram company), Wire Belt Company, and Nercon are well-known. The largest players vary by conveyor type and industry, a company that dominates warehouse sortation may not build sanitary food conveyors.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How do I choose a conveyor manufacturer?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Start by defining your application requirements: product type, weight, speed, environment (washdown, cleanroom, outdoor), and any regulatory standards (FDA, USDA, ATEX). Then evaluate manufacturers based on relevant experience in your industry, geographic proximity for service and installation, lead time, warranty terms, parts availability, and controls integration capability. Request references from similar installations and ask for a factory visit or demo if possible.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between a conveyor manufacturer and an integrator?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A conveyor manufacturer designs and builds conveyor equipment, they fabricate frames, assemble drives, and ship the physical hardware. An integrator designs the overall material handling system, selects equipment from one or more manufacturers, handles controls and software, manages installation, and commissions the complete system. Some companies do both; others specialize in one role. For simple single-conveyor purchases, buying direct from a manufacturer is common. For complex multi-zone systems, an integrator adds value through system design and project management.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">Do conveyor manufacturers sell direct or through distributors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It varies by company. Some manufacturers like Dorner and mk North America sell both direct and through distributor networks. Others like Hytrol sell primarily through a network of integration partners. Large system manufacturers like Dematic and Honeywell Intelligrated typically sell direct through their own sales engineering teams. For packaging-specific conveyors, many manufacturers sell direct because the applications require custom engineering. Always check the manufacturer's website for their preferred sales channel in your region.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">Can I buy conveyor parts from any manufacturer?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Many conveyor components, rollers, bearings, motors, VFDs, belting, and chain, are available from third-party suppliers and are not proprietary to the conveyor manufacturer. However, some manufacturers use proprietary frame profiles, belt lacing systems, or controller software that requires OEM parts. Before purchasing a conveyor, ask the manufacturer what percentage of wear parts are standard catalog items versus proprietary components. This affects long-term maintenance costs and parts availability.</p>

</div>
</div>
